<section class="almanac-section mental-model-section">
  <div class="shell mental-model-grid">
    <div class="mental-model-copy">
      <p class="almanac-kicker">The 90-second tour</p>
      <h2>Six facts explain Dekopon.</h2>
      <p>These are the boundaries every deeper detail preserves.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="mental-model-list">
      <li><span>01</span><div><strong>The request starts with a subject.</strong><p>A chat transport supplies an authenticated sender. The local console uses an explicit opt-in development subject. Neither is already a principal.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>02</span><div><strong>The broker owns identity.</strong><p>It authenticates the gateway, checks its attestor grant, and alone maps subject to principal.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>03</span><div><strong>The model gets one tool.</strong><p>Its homemade shell can explore and combine granted capabilities but cannot reach the operating system.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>04</span><div><strong>Cedar permits; constraint sets limit.</strong><p>Policy chooses who may act. Separate configuration chooses how far the action can reach.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>05</span><div><strong>Credentials stay with the broker.</strong><p>The native HTTP engine injects a destination-bound value after provider headers are checked.</p></div></li>
      <li><span>06</span><div><strong>Audit, traces, and logs answer different questions.</strong><p>Authorization history stays durable while OpenTelemetry explains flow, timing, cost, and failures.</p></div></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

